# Aarogya Emergency Safety Rules

## Emergency Priority

During an emergency, the application prioritizes:

1. Immediate safety instruction
2. Current emergency state
3. User location
4. Critical health information
5. Communication state
6. Battery state
7. Secondary information

Do not allow decorative UI to compete with emergency information.

## Disaster Activation

Disaster Mode may be activated through:

• Official warning
• Supported local detection
• User activation

Local detection must only be implemented for scenarios where there is a technically defensible signal.

Never claim universal automatic disaster detection.

## Disaster Specific Guidance

Guidance must depend on the disaster type.

Flood:

• Move toward a suitable safer location.
• Avoid moving water.
• Avoid flooded electrical areas.
• Follow known evacuation guidance where available.

Do not tell users that higher altitude is automatically safe.

Fire:

• Prioritize evacuation.
• Avoid smoke exposure.
• Avoid elevators where appropriate.
• Follow known evacuation routes.

Heat wave:

• Reduce unnecessary physical exertion.
• Move to a cooler safe location.
• Hydrate when appropriate and safe.
• Monitor changing physical condition.

Cyclone:

• Follow official evacuation instructions.
• Stay away from windows and exposed areas.
• Remain in a structurally safer location.

These instructions must be reviewed before being presented as production emergency guidance.

## Offline Operation

When internet is unavailable:

Continue:

• Local inference
• Local monitoring
• Local emergency state
• Local event creation
• Local event storage
• Previously downloaded guidance
• Previously downloaded maps
• Local communication where available

Clearly show connectivity state.

## Emergency Packets

Emergency packets should contain minimum necessary information.

Possible fields:

• Event type
• Timestamp
• Location if permitted
• Relevant health readings
• Risk level
• Battery state
• Last known movement
• Device identifier where necessary

Do not attach the user's entire health history.

## Emergency Packet States

Use explicit states:

CREATED
STORED
QUEUED
TRANSMITTING
SENT
DELIVERY_CONFIRMED
FAILED

Never show:

"Emergency sent"

unless delivery is actually confirmed.

If stored locally:

"Emergency event saved on this device."

## Store and Forward

If communication is unavailable:

Create event
→ store locally
→ monitor connectivity
→ queue event
→ attempt transmission
→ confirm delivery

Retry logic must avoid sending duplicate events unnecessarily.

Use event IDs for deduplication.

## Local Communication

Local device communication should be designed behind an abstraction.

Possible technologies:

• BLE
• Wi-Fi Direct
• Other supported local transports

Messages should have:

• Unique ID
• Timestamp
• Origin
• Hop information where required
• Expiry
• Integrity verification where appropriate

Prevent indefinite message forwarding.

## Battery

Disaster and Survival Mode must consider battery.

Avoid unnecessary:

• Network scans
• GPS polling
• Screen updates
• Sensor sampling
• Model inference

Critical monitoring should receive priority.

## Fall Confirmation

When a possible fall occurs:

1. Detect possible fall.
2. Notify user.
3. Allow a response period.
4. If no response and conditions support escalation, create an emergency event.
5. Continue monitoring.

Do not immediately declare every acceleration spike an emergency.

## Location

Location must require appropriate permission.

If location is unavailable:

Do not invent it.

Display:

"Location unavailable."

The system may use the last known location only when clearly labeled.

## Emergency Claims

Never guarantee:

• Rescue
• Medical treatment
• Network availability
• Emergency response time
• Survival

Aarogya assists the user and provides information.

It does not control emergency services.

## Human Safety

If the system is uncertain, it should communicate uncertainty.

Examples:

"Possible fall detected."

"Possible elevated risk detected."

"Connectivity unavailable. Emergency event saved locally."

Never provide false certainty.
